k at 2560-2593. Let me read it to see what exists and whether it covers this case.
This is the breakthrough insight: the existing chokepoint guards all use a **racy content-compare** to distinguish stale-clobber from legit-write. But the acquire-evict already KNOWS which blocks are stale — when it SKIPS an in_ail block (can't clear DONE), it sets bgen=0 but leaves it DONE, and xfsaild then pushes it (sess28: in_ail=1 bgen=0). The clean fix: have the evict set a **positive "evict-stale" marker**, and the write chokepoint skips exactly those (no content compare). This distinguishes stale-clobber from fresh-init/legit without the racy compare. Let me check the buffer fields.
Let me read the evict skip branch precisely to place the marker correctly.
Precise root now identified: the dir read-time gen-invalidation hook (`xfs_da_read_buf`) **skips in_ail/dirty/pinned buffers** (can't clear DONE safely — corruption), so a dir MODIFY that reads a stale prior-tenure in_ail block RMWs the stale base (bgen=0, missing peer's dirent) → durable loss. Let me examine the read hook to see if a stale **destaged** in_ail block (content already on disk → safe to re-read) can be invalidated, closing part of the residual.
The read hook is very mature — it already invalidates **destaged** in_ail stale blocks (sess133 undestaged discriminator). Crucially, there's a gated `dir_evict_prior_tenure`/`dir_tenure_evict` lever (epoch-based) that invalidates prior-tenure stale blocks even past the keep-guard — and it was **NOT in the winning config**. This is a promising untested-in-this-config lever for exactly the stale-base RMW. Let me test it at 8 nodes (reliable failure) via the prefix.
